      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "\\[(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = 100x^2 + 120x + 20.\\]\nThe expression \\(100x^2 + 120x + 20\\) can be factored as\n\\[100x^2 + 120x + 20 = 50(2x^2 + 2.4x + 0.4).\\]\nSince \\(100x^2 + 120x + 20\\) is a product of three multiples of 10 and an odd number, the expression is divisible by the highest power of any prime factor multiplied by 5. Of all numbers \\(2x^2 + 2.4x + 0.4\\), the highest power of prime factors applicable are 2, 5, and 7. However, the term \\(2x^2\\) contains both 2 and 5, \\(0.4 = \\frac{2}{5}\\) contains \\(\\frac{2}{5}\\), and the terms \\(2.4x\\) contain \\(2x\\), which forms the value 2 or 4 depending on \\(x\\). Hence, we need to examine divisibility by 2 and 5 factors that always hold for any odd \\(x\\).\nSince \\(50(2x^2 + 2.4x + 0.4)\\) equals 2 \\(\\times\\) 5 \\(\\times\\) 10 \\(\\times\\) \\(2x^2 + 2.4x + 0.4\\) and an odd number, the value only depends on \\(2x^2 + 2.4x + 0.4\\) evaluated at \\(x = 1\\).\nLet's solve \\(2x^2 + 2.4x + 0.4\\) at \\(x = 1\\):\n$$\n2(1)^2 + 2.4(1) + 0.4 = 2 + 2.4 + 0.4 = 4.8 \\neq 0.\n$$\nThis implies that \\(2x^2 + 2.4x + 0.4\\) never equals zero, so there is no odd number \\(x\\) for which the expression \\(100x^2 + 120x + 20\\) would be exactly zero.\nThe higher powers 5 and 7 do not apply because none of the greater powers of \\(x\\) are 5 or 7, respectively.\nThus, there will always be a number, representing as \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
